<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Three independent Keycloak SPI providers, one per case-study org, for chapter 39 (Writing Custom
  SPIs: Authenticators, Event Listeners, Protocol Mappers) of the IAM & Keycloak Mastery course:

    - com.meridiantrust.keycloak.businesshours   - a custom Authenticator (Meridian Trust)
    - com.riversidehealth.keycloak.loginaudit     - a custom EventListenerProvider (Riverside Health)
    - com.northwindapps.keycloak.tenantmapper     - a custom ProtocolMapper (Northwind Apps)

  Built as ONE Maven project producing ONE jar, not three separate modules. Rationale (see the
  chapter text for the fuller version): all three SPIs are small, have zero dependencies on each
  other, and share the identical build/deploy mechanics (mvn package, bind-mount into
  /opt/keycloak/providers, kc.sh build, restart) that chapter 33 already established for a single
  provider. Three separate Maven modules would mean three separate JARs, three separate
  docker-compose bind mounts, and three separate "did I rebuild before restarting" failure points for
  no real isolation benefit, since nothing here needs independent versioning or independent
  deployment. Each provider still gets its own Java package (one per case-study org) so the source
  tree itself documents the separation that matters: which org's problem each class solves.

  Dependency coordinates were live-verified against Maven Central and against the actual jars bundled
  inside the running quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:26.8.2 image before this POM was written, the same
  method chapter 33 used (see the chapter text for the full trail: a docker run against the image's
  own /opt/keycloak/lib, cross-checked with repo1.maven.org). Four Keycloak artifacts are needed,
  all pinned to 26.8.2, all confirmed present on Maven Central at that exact version, and all
  confirmed to be the exact jars this course's own Keycloak image bundles - no nearest-version
  substitute needed for any of them:

    - keycloak-server-spi          Provider, ProviderFactory, ConfiguredProvider, ProviderConfigProperty,
                                    KeycloakSession, RealmModel, UserModel, ProtocolMapperModel,
                                    UserSessionModel, ClientSessionContext, AuthenticationExecutionModel
    - keycloak-server-spi-private   Authenticator, AuthenticatorFactory, ConfigurableAuthenticatorFactory,
                                    AuthenticationFlowContext, AuthenticationFlowError, EventListenerProvider,
                                    EventListenerProviderFactory, Event, EventType, Errors, EventBuilder,
                                    ProtocolMapper (the base interface - NOT its OIDC-specific
                                    subinterfaces, which live one jar over, see below)
    - keycloak-services             AbstractOIDCProtocolMapper, OIDCAccessTokenMapper, OIDCIDTokenMapper,
                                    UserInfoTokenMapper, OIDCAttributeMapperHelper - the OIDC-specific
                                    half of the Protocol Mapper SPI, confirmed NOT present in either
                                    server-spi jar (a real, confirmed split across jars, worth knowing
                                    before guessing a dependency list for this SPI from its name alone)
    - keycloak-core                  AccessToken, IDToken, AccessTokenResponse, JsonWebToken (the token
                                    representation classes AbstractOIDCProtocolMapper's methods operate
                                    on; a transitive dependency of keycloak-services, declared explicitly
                                    here anyway for the same reason chapter 33 declared its two
                                    dependencies explicitly rather than relying on transitivity)

  All four are scope=provided: the server supplies these exact classes at runtime from its own
  /opt/keycloak/lib, so this provider's own JAR should contain only this chapter's own classes plus
  its service-registration files - nothing from Keycloak itself (bundling them instead risks a real
  classloading conflict, per chapter 33's own "common mistakes" section).
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <groupId>org.iamkeycloakmastery</groupId>
  <artifactId>custom-spis</artifactId>
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>IAM &amp; Keycloak Mastery - Chapter 39 Custom SPIs</name>
  <description>
    A custom Authenticator (Meridian Trust business-hours gate), a custom EventListenerProvider
    (Riverside Health failed-login audit log), and a custom ProtocolMapper (Northwind Apps tenant_id
    claim), for chapter 39 of the IAM &amp; Keycloak Mastery course.
  </description>

  <properties>
    <maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    <!-- Pinned to the exact Keycloak version this lab's docker-compose.yml runs. -->
    <keycloak.version>26.8.2</keycloak.version>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.keycloak</groupId>
      <artifactId>keycloak-server-spi</artifactId>
      <version>${keycloak.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.keycloak</groupId>
      <artifactId>keycloak-server-spi-private</artifactId>
      <version>${keycloak.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.keycloak</groupId>
      <artifactId>keycloak-services</artifactId>
      <version>${keycloak.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.keycloak</groupId>
      <artifactId>keycloak-core</artifactId>
      <version>${keycloak.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <finalName>custom-spis-${project.version}</finalName>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.13.0</version>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.4.1</version>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
